# The commit-subject rule, in ONE place (#15473).
#
# Two halves of this rule already disagreed once: the branch commits were
# checked and the squash subject was not, so `fix(deps+security): ...` merged
# and put a permanently non-conforming subject on main. The fix added a PR-title
# gate in Python, and a second copy of the pattern would have reintroduced the
# same class of defect one layer up -- two enforcers of one rule, free to drift.
#
# Format: comment lines (#) and blank lines are ignored; exactly one remaining
# line, which is a POSIX ERE. Both readers fail closed on anything else, because
# a subject rule that cannot be loaded must not read as a subject that passes.
#
# Readers:
#   scripts/lint-conventions.sh   -- grep -qE, commit-msg and range modes
#   scripts/validate_pr_body.py   -- re.match, the PR-title gate
#
# Why the classes are this wide (#14076): the repo writes slashed scopes
# (`fix(llc/frontend):`), comma-joined scopes (`docs(architecture,design):`) and
# hyphenated types (`tech-debt:`, `a11y:`). The scope must still START
# alphanumeric -- `fix(/llc):` and `fix(-llc):` are not scopes anyone intends.
^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*(\([a-z0-9][a-z0-9._/,-]*\))?: .+
